    (5, None, '\'It\'s fine, you\'ll get along.\' "Podruzhites" is built on "drug", friend: you\'ll become friends with each other.'),
    (6, None, '\'Kostya, tell the story about the strawberry mojito.\' "Rasskazhi" is "tell" to one friend. "Klubnichnyy", strawberry, comes from "klubnika".'),
    (7, None, '\'And the one about that bald guy.\' "Pro" is the chatty word for "about". "Lysyy" means bald, and "togo lysogo" is "that bald one".'),
    (8, None, '\'That never happened.\' "Takogo ne bylo nikogda" is literally "of such there wasn\'t never". The double "no" is ordinary Russian.'),
    (9, None, '\'For some reason, I\'m not surprised.\' "Pochemu-to" is "why" with "-to" added: "for some reason". "Udivlena" ends in "-a" because a woman is speaking.'),
    (10, None, '\'Anyway, you stay a while, and we\'ll probably go.\' "Posidite" is literally "sit a bit", the usual way to tell people to stay on. "A my" means "and as for us".'),
    (11, None, '\'It\'s time for us to go home.\' "Nam pora" is literally "to us, it\'s time". "Domoy" means "homewards", unlike "doma", at home.'),
    (12, None, '\'Since at work it\'s forbidden for us.\' "A to" here means "because otherwise". "Zapreshcheno", forbidden, is Vika\'s own rule from the morning.'),
    (13, None, '\'Guys, what\'s got into you?\' "Rebyata" is "guys" for any group of friends. "Vy chego?" is a casual "what\'s up with you?".'),
    (14, None, '\'No, you stay here and have fun, and I\'ll go.\' "Ostavaytes" and "veselites" are both commands for the group, and both end in "-tes".'),
